<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reapers' War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1640">1640</a>–<a href="/years/1659">1659</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Catalan Revolt · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-spanish-war-1635-1659">Franco-Spanish War (1635–1659)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reapers' War was a revolt of Catalonia against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a>. Provoked by the billeting of royal troops and the policies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares</a>, it began with the killing of the viceroy by reapers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in June 1640. The Catalans placed themselves under the French crown, but Barcelona surrendered in 1652, and in 1659 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reapers-war.jpg" alt="Reapers' War" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pandolfo Reschi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Montju%C3%AFc_de_1641.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war came out of Catalan resentment at the royal army, made up mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of different nations, which was quartered on Catalan peasants during the Franco-Spanish War of 1635–1659. Peasants reported sacrilege, destruction of property and rape by the soldiers and answered with local rebellions. At the same time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares</a>, chief minister of Philip IV, pursued his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arms">Union of Arms</a> to spread the burden of the empire more evenly. Catalan institutions resisted, and the Courts of 1626 and 1632 were never concluded because the measures violated the Constitutions of Catalonia. In 1638 the canon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pau-claris">Pau Claris</a> was elected president of the Generalitat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The revolt came to a head on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> in 1640 with the uprising called Bloody Corpus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. It began with the death of a reaper and ended with the killing of the viceroy, the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmau-de-queralt-count-of-santa-coloma">Count of Santa Coloma</a>. Claris summoned the Junta de Braços, which set up councils of justice, defence and treasury and sought French help. Under a pact at Ceret in September 1640 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> promised support.</p><p>On 17 January 1641 the Catalan Republic was proclaimed under French protection, but after the defeat at Martorell on 23 January the Catalans recognised Louis XIII as Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. A Franco-Catalan victory at Montjuïc on 26 January 1641 followed, and Claris died a month later, probably poisoned by Spanish agents. A personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was ratified at Peronne on 19 September 1641.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>French control of Catalan affairs gradually undermined enthusiasm for the alliance. The Franco-Catalan attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> failed, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> fell after a ten-month siege and all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> came under French control. Famine, plague, Philip IV's 1644 promise to respect Catalan institutions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">Fronde</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1652 after a year's siege. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659 ceded Roussillon and the northern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> to France. The war also helped Portugal win its Restoration War, since Spanish troops were busy in Catalonia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reapers' War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The valleys kept out of the wars that repeatedly crossed the Pyrenees, including the <b>Reapers' War</b> of 1640–1652, the War of the Spanish Succession and the Napoleonic wars, partly by their isolation and partly by respecting both co-princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Catalonia rose against Philip IV in 1640 in the <b>Reapers' War</b>, provoked by the billeting of royal troops and the Count-Duke of Olivares's demands.</blockquote><a href="/state/catalan-republic-1641">Catalan Republic (1641)</a></li></ul></section>
